-- router.szh
-- The router: control element above the substrate's components.
--
-- Engineering reframe: the unified equation {xi; (*zeta, *x)R.k}
-- : {omega, delta_rk} is a closed-loop feedback control system
-- at full generality. xi is the controlled variable's current
-- state; zeta is the measurement of deviation from setpoint;
-- R.k is the corrective transformation; omega is the new state;
-- delta_rk is the residual that drives the next loop.
--
-- The substrate has process units (kernel cascade, journey
-- cascade, polynomial composition) but no controller. Inputs
-- arrive without dispatch logic; outputs are produced without
-- a feedback path. The router fills that gap. It measures the
-- input's task type and structural characteristics, looks up
-- the operating range of each available component, and
-- dispatches to the component whose operating range contains
-- the input. Inputs outside all components' operating ranges
-- produce graceful degradation (explicit failure with
-- explanation, not silent wrong output).
--
-- The router unblocks the user-surface (submit_query has
-- something to call) and shifts the empirical program from
-- hypothesis-falsification mode to operating-range-
-- characterization mode. The cascades stop being competitors
-- for a single best representation and become characterized
-- components in a dispatched system.

-- Step 1: characterize a component's operating range. Run the
-- component against representative inputs across the test
-- surface; measure where reliability degrades; record the
-- boundary as a range specification. This journey runs at
-- characterization time (during empirical-program probes); the
-- output feeds the dispatch journey at deployment time.
journey characterize_operating_range
  xi: component_with_test_inputs_and_metrics
  zeta: empirical_program_with_corpus_test_surface
  rk: run_component_then_measure_then_classify_range_boundary
  omega: operating_range_specification_with_input_characteristics
  terminus: range_recorded_in_router_table
  failure_modes: insufficient_test_data_or_metrics_unstable

-- Step 2: route an input to the matching component. The
-- controller's primary action. Measure the input's task type
-- and structural characteristics; check each registered
-- component's operating range; dispatch to the first matching
-- component or fall through to graceful degradation.
journey route_input
  xi: input_with_task_type_and_text
  zeta: router_table_with_component_operating_ranges
  rk: characterize_input_then_match_then_dispatch
  omega: component_output_with_dispatch_record
  terminus: output_returned_to_caller
  failure_modes: no_component_operating_range_matches_input

-- Step 3: graceful degradation. When no component's operating
-- range contains the input, return an explicit failure with
-- explanation rather than producing silent wrong output. The
-- user (or upstream caller) receives a typed failure naming
-- which characteristics of the input fell outside known
-- ranges, so they can either rephrase the input or treat the
-- system's limit as the answer.
journey report_outside_range
  xi: input_outside_all_component_operating_ranges
  zeta: router_table_with_failure_explanation_templates
  rk: identify_unmatched_characteristics_then_format_failure
  omega: typed_failure_with_explanation_and_unmatched_axes
  terminus: failure_returned_to_caller
  failure_modes: explanation_template_missing_for_unmatched_axis

-- The composing program — the closed-loop controller for the
-- substrate. Parallel composition because characterize and
-- route can run concurrently (characterize feeds the table
-- that route reads), but a given input flows serially through
-- route → (component | report_outside_range).
program rae_router_program
  prescribes: [characterize_operating_range, route_input, report_outside_range]
  rk: 1
  composition: parallel
